{"type":"bridge-session","sessionId":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","bridgeSessionId":"cse_01P6EtEZrtka8BvMWBZeka4V","lastSequenceNum":0}
{"parentUuid":"c0102c40-daef-4589-b28a-8e58dd128bc0","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CdgH7xQ7z8HdzdDTe8Apt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015Vq2ovzR3QtiwMi72QSs4j","name":"ExitPlanMode","input":{"plan":"# Opus to −25% by porting S1 — the best fair config, never run on Opus\n\n## Context\n\nGoal: Opus ≥ −25% on the 20-instance official-300 sample. Current fair number **−11.2%**\n[−27.8, +9.6] (n=20, published prompt, both arms symmetric, cap 120).\n\nI stopped proposing new mechanisms and mined the existing cells. Two things came out of that, and\ntogether they change what to run.\n\n### 1. Every payload-defect hypothesis is dead — verified, not assumed\n\nA 5-agent forensic sweep read all 20 sessions and produced 38 candidate defects. Adversarial\nverifiers with a **winner-control** requirement refuted **27 of 28** adjudicated so far:\n\n| claimed defect | winner control that killed it |\n|---|---|\n| bodies printed with no line numbers (prompt promises them) | **20/20 instances** — 6/6 winners, incl. the biggest wins |\n| `impact` blind-chained to rank-1, no gate | off-gold-file target in **7/12 winners**; the two largest wins have it in *more* degenerate form |\n| query polluted by GitHub issue-template boilerplate | 2/6 control winners have boilerplate queries → boilerplate top hits |\n| hits printed out of score order | 5/20 payloads, incl. winners lucene (−52%) and vuejs (−24%) |\n| line-less fabricated `server.h` caller | redis-10095 (+65) **and valkey-1842 (−21%, a winner)** |\n| `eg-q.run` path exposed 5× in prompt | 20/20 |\n\nOnly survivor: the shared **GOMODCACHE leak** (caddy's *baseline* read the fix out of\n`~/go/pkg/mod/…caddy/v2@v2.11.4/replacer.go`, 27 of 66 calls) — and it explains a winner, not a loss.\n\n**Conclusion: the tool is equally broken everywhere. Payload quality does not decide the sign.**\nDo not spend further effort on payload defects.\n\n### 2. Tokens are a pure function of the op-count ratio, and eg's op count is invariant\n\n| config | baseline ops | eg ops | ops Δ | tokens |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| G40 rich/rich, official | **23.8** | 28.7 | +21.1% | +28.4% |\n| OFF bare/rich, official | **39.0** | 27.5 | −29.6% | −46.5% |\n| PUB none/capability, published | **35.7** | 31.7 | −11.2% | −11.2% |\n\neg lands at 27.5 / 28.7 / 31.7 regardless of prompt. The **baseline** swings 23.8 → 39.0 purely on how\nmuch methodology its prompt carries. So the withdrawn −46.5% was a crippled control, and G40's +28.4%\nwas a control given better discipline than eg. Neither was about the tool.\n\n### 3. What the winning cells do that no Opus cell has ever done\n\nThe tool's job is to collapse the locate phase. On Haiku's best fair cells it halves pre-edit locate.\nOn Opus it never has:\n\n| cell | pre-edit base → eg | ops Δ | tokens |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **haiku S1** (n=50) | **16.2 → 8.5 (−48%)** | −39.3% | **−39.9%** |\n| haiku RR (n=21) | 14.2 → 6.3 (−56%) | −46.7% | −44.7% |\n| haiku R30 (n=30) | 15.7 → 10.3 (−34%) | −32.8% | −42.9% |\n| sonnet R30 (n=30) | 7.0 → 6.4 (−9%) | −25.1% | −24.5% |\n| **opus PUB** | **13.4 → 14.6 (+9%)** | −11.2% | −11.2% |\n| opus OFF | 19.1 → 13.6 (−29%) | −29.6% | −46.5% |\n\nOpus's baseline pre-edit (13.4) is comparable to Haiku's (16.2), so this is **not** \"Opus explores\nless\". It is that the config which halves pre-edit has only ever been run on Haiku.\n\n**S1 = `PROMPT_FAMILY=vendor BASE_CLAMP=1 EG_GATED_CLAMP=1`.** The harness's own comment on that\ncombination: *\"the matched control: a bare clamp on both sides, no shared block on either, so the only\ndifference is search-tool vs shell.\"* I verified its symmetry earlier by line-diff — 384 vs 370 words,\nmirrored clause for clause (LOCATE-FIRST/SEARCH-FIRST, READ the range, EDIT, do-not-re-confirm, STOP,\ntwo-attempts-then-widen). It is the best fair result in the whole program and **has never been run on\nOpus.**\n\n## Plan\n\n### 1. Run S1 on Opus, same 20 instances — 40 sessions\n`PROMPT_FAMILY=vendor BASE_CLAMP=1 EG_GATED_CLAMP=1`, `CC_MODEL=opus CLAUDE_EFFORT=high`,\n`TASKS_FILE=tasks/opus20_official.json`, binary `/tmp/eg-f19`, fresh `--cache-dir`.\n\nDeliberately **no prefetch** (S1 didn't use it; and the Arm A probe showed prefetch removal raises\ngraph usage 0.20→3.00) and **`GQ_SEARCH_CAP=0`** so follow-ups are possible. Cap 120 and\n`CC_TIMEOUT=3600` to keep `CENSORED=0` — the PUB baseline averaged 50.2 turns, so a 50-cap censors the\nbaseline specifically.\n\n### 2. Fairness gate before scoring — on emitted prompts, not config\nLine-diff the two arms with paths/digits normalised and account for every differing line; run the\nclause table (LOCATE/SEARCH-FIRST, READ range, EDIT, do-not-re-confirm, STOP, fallback) and require\n**parity or baseline-favouring**. S1's blocks passed this before; confirm they still do under the\ncurrent `run_3arm.sh`.\n\n### 3. Leading indicator, checkable before spending on the full score\n**Pre-edit locate must fall below the baseline's.** It is +9% today; every cell that reached ≤ −25%\ntokens had it at −34% or better. If pre-edit does not go negative by ~8 completed pairs, stop the cell\n— that is the mechanism, and without it the tokens cannot follow.\n\n### 4. Fix the one confirmed integrity defect in the same run\nIsolate `GOMODCACHE`/`GOPATH` per instance (and the npm/gem equivalents) so no arm can read a\npost-fix upstream release. Without this, caddy's baseline is reading the answer.\n\n### 5. Pre-registered\n- Success = geomean ≤ −25% with a CI upper bound below zero.\n- Arithmetic from the current cell: 12 winners contribute ln-sum −5.369, 8 losers +2.996. Fixing all 8\n  losers to parity gives only −23.5%, so S1 must also **deepen the winners** — which is exactly what a\n  pre-edit halving does, and why this config rather than a loser-targeted patch.\n- Falsification: if S1 on Opus lands within noise of −11.2%, then the pre-edit halving is\n  Haiku-specific and **−25% on Opus is not reachable with any config in this program.** I will report\n  that rather than keep re-cutting the same data.\n\n### 6. Replicate before claiming\nSecond independent cell if it clears. Three configs today looked strong at n≤12 and reversed by n=20.\n\n## Verification\n- `tools/model_cmp.py`; geomean of per-instance ratios + bootstrap CI; cost classes separately.\n- Gate: `total_cost_usd > 0` AND non-empty `patch.diff`, dropped symmetrically.\n- `CENSORED` must stay 0 per arm.\n- Report ops (pre/post/build) alongside tokens — ops predicted tokens within 3pt in all three cells\n  above, so it is the cheap early read on whether the cell is working.\n- Verify on emitted artifacts, never config — ten silent plumbing failures this session, the worst a\n  flag checked in one dispatcher but not the one every cell uses.\n\n## Files\n- `/tmp/mcptest/s1worker.sh` (new) — the S1 flag set, Opus, cap 120.\n- `tools/run_3arm.sh` — no changes needed; `base_clamp_prompt` and `gated_clamp_eg_prompt` already\n  exist and are what S1 used.\n- `tools/model_cmp.py` — scorer, already reproduces every historical number.\n- **No product change.** `eg-consolidated` stays clean at HEAD `024f25a`; every payload-defect\n  hypothesis that would have motivated one is refuted.\n\n## Honest expectation\nThis is a port, not a theory: the config with the best fair measurement in the program, applied to the\nmodel that has never had it, with a mechanism (pre-edit halving) that is measured on 50 instances and\nabsent on Opus. That is the strongest remaining basis for the goal.\n\nThe risk is real though. Opus may simply not respond to a locate-first clamp the way Haiku does — its\nsessions are dominated by reproduce-and-verify work that the graph cannot touch, and Sonnet's own S1-\nfamily cell reached only −24.5% with pre-edit barely moving (−9%). So I would put this at\n**−20% to −35%**, wide, with a genuine chance it lands near −11% and settles the question negatively.\nThe leading indicator in step 3 means we find out cheaply rather than after 40 sessions.\n","planFilePath":"/Users/suhaan/.claude/plans/modular-cooking-cupcake.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4712,"cache_read_input_tokens":512241,"output_tokens":31,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4712,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":31,"cache_read_input_tokens":512241,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4712,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4712},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdgH7q5q7ofFkryVtpi1X","type":"assistant","uuid":"bf49f8d2-c8fa-4bbc-9616-513878690e4e","timestamp":"2026-08-03T18:21:06.571Z","effort":"xhigh","session_id":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"modular-cooking-cupcake"}
{"parentUuid":"bf49f8d2-c8fa-4bbc-9616-513878690e4e","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"0813e2c2-9e63-40ae-82ae-61cde2f8fc2a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","content":"User has approved your plan. You can now start coding. Start with updating your todo list if applicable\n\nYour plan has been saved to: /Users/suhaan/.claude/plans/modular-cooking-cupcake.md\nYou can refer back to it if needed during implementation.\n\n## Approved Plan:\n# Opus to −25% by porting S1 — the best fair config, never run on Opus\n\n## Context\n\nGoal: Opus ≥ −25% on the 20-instance official-300 sample. Current fair number **−11.2%**\n[−27.8, +9.6] (n=20, published prompt, both arms symmetric, cap 120).\n\nI stopped proposing new mechanisms and mined the existing cells. Two things came out of that, and\ntogether they change what to run.\n\n### 1. Every payload-defect hypothesis is dead — verified, not assumed\n\nA 5-agent forensic sweep read all 20 sessions and produced 38 candidate defects. Adversarial\nverifiers with a **winner-control** requirement refuted **27 of 28** adjudicated so far:\n\n| claimed defect | winner control that killed it |\n|---|---|\n| bodies printed with no line numbers (prompt promises them) | **20/20 instances** — 6/6 winners, incl. the biggest wins |\n| `impact` blind-chained to rank-1, no gate | off-gold-file target in **7/12 winners**; the two largest wins have it in *more* degenerate form |\n| query polluted by GitHub issue-template boilerplate | 2/6 control winners have boilerplate queries → boilerplate top hits |\n| hits printed out of score order | 5/20 payloads, incl. winners lucene (−52%) and vuejs (−24%) |\n| line-less fabricated `server.h` caller | redis-10095 (+65) **and valkey-1842 (−21%, a winner)** |\n| `eg-q.run` path exposed 5× in prompt | 20/20 |\n\nOnly survivor: the shared **GOMODCACHE leak** (caddy's *baseline* read the fix out of\n`~/go/pkg/mod/…caddy/v2@v2.11.4/replacer.go`, 27 of 66 calls) — and it explains a winner, not a loss.\n\n**Conclusion: the tool is equally broken everywhere. Payload quality does not decide the sign.**\nDo not spend further effort on payload defects.\n\n### 2. Tokens are a pure function of the op-count ratio, and eg's op count is invariant\n\n| config | baseline ops | eg ops | ops Δ | tokens |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| G40 rich/rich, official | **23.8** | 28.7 | +21.1% | +28.4% |\n| OFF bare/rich, official | **39.0** | 27.5 | −29.6% | −46.5% |\n| PUB none/capability, published | **35.7** | 31.7 | −11.2% | −11.2% |\n\neg lands at 27.5 / 28.7 / 31.7 regardless of prompt. The **baseline** swings 23.8 → 39.0 purely on how\nmuch methodology its prompt carries. So the withdrawn −46.5% was a crippled control, and G40's +28.4%\nwas a control given better discipline than eg. Neither was about the tool.\n\n### 3. What the winning cells do that no Opus cell has ever done\n\nThe tool's job is to collapse the locate phase. On Haiku's best fair cells it halves pre-edit locate.\nOn Opus it never has:\n\n| cell | pre-edit base → eg | ops Δ | tokens |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **haiku S1** (n=50) | **16.2 → 8.5 (−48%)** | −39.3% | **−39.9%** |\n| haiku RR (n=21) | 14.2 → 6.3 (−56%) | −46.7% | −44.7% |\n| haiku R30 (n=30) | 15.7 → 10.3 (−34%) | −32.8% | −42.9% |\n| sonnet R30 (n=30) | 7.0 → 6.4 (−9%) | −25.1% | −24.5% |\n| **opus PUB** | **13.4 → 14.6 (+9%)** | −11.2% | −11.2% |\n| opus OFF | 19.1 → 13.6 (−29%) | −29.6% | −46.5% |\n\nOpus's baseline pre-edit (13.4) is comparable to Haiku's (16.2), so this is **not** \"Opus explores\nless\". It is that the config which halves pre-edit has only ever been run on Haiku.\n\n**S1 = `PROMPT_FAMILY=vendor BASE_CLAMP=1 EG_GATED_CLAMP=1`.** The harness's own comment on that\ncombination: *\"the matched control: a bare clamp on both sides, no shared block on either, so the only\ndifference is search-tool vs shell.\"* I verified its symmetry earlier by line-diff — 384 vs 370 words,\nmirrored clause for clause (LOCATE-FIRST/SEARCH-FIRST, READ the range, EDIT, do-not-re-confirm, STOP,\ntwo-attempts-then-widen). It is the best fair result in the whole program and **has never been run on\nOpus.**\n\n## Plan\n\n### 1. Run S1 on Opus, same 20 instances — 40 sessions\n`PROMPT_FAMILY=vendor BASE_CLAMP=1 EG_GATED_CLAMP=1`, `CC_MODEL=opus CLAUDE_EFFORT=high`,\n`TASKS_FILE=tasks/opus20_official.json`, binary `/tmp/eg-f19`, fresh `--cache-dir`.\n\nDeliberately **no prefetch** (S1 didn't use it; and the Arm A probe showed prefetch removal raises\ngraph usage 0.20→3.00) and **`GQ_SEARCH_CAP=0`** so follow-ups are possible. Cap 120 and\n`CC_TIMEOUT=3600` to keep `CENSORED=0` — the PUB baseline averaged 50.2 turns, so a 50-cap censors the\nbaseline specifically.\n\n### 2. Fairness gate before scoring — on emitted prompts, not config\nLine-diff the two arms with paths/digits normalised and account for every differing line; run the\nclause table (LOCATE/SEARCH-FIRST, READ range, EDIT, do-not-re-confirm, STOP, fallback) and require\n**parity or baseline-favouring**. S1's blocks passed this before; confirm they still do under the\ncurrent `run_3arm.sh`.\n\n### 3. Leading indicator, checkable before spending on the full score\n**Pre-edit locate must fall below the baseline's.** It is +9% today; every cell that reached ≤ −25%\ntokens had it at −34% or better. If pre-edit does not go negative by ~8 completed pairs, stop the cell\n— that is the mechanism, and without it the tokens cannot follow.\n\n### 4. Fix the one confirmed integrity defect in the same run\nIsolate `GOMODCACHE`/`GOPATH` per instance (and the npm/gem equivalents) so no arm can read a\npost-fix upstream release. Without this, caddy's baseline is reading the answer.\n\n### 5. Pre-registered\n- Success = geomean ≤ −25% with a CI upper bound below zero.\n- Arithmetic from the current cell: 12 winners contribute ln-sum −5.369, 8 losers +2.996. Fixing all 8\n  losers to parity gives only −23.5%, so S1 must also **deepen the winners** — which is exactly what a\n  pre-edit halving does, and why this config rather than a loser-targeted patch.\n- Falsification: if S1 on Opus lands within noise of −11.2%, then the pre-edit halving is\n  Haiku-specific and **−25% on Opus is not reachable with any config in this program.** I will report\n  that rather than keep re-cutting the same data.\n\n### 6. Replicate before claiming\nSecond independent cell if it clears. Three configs today looked strong at n≤12 and reversed by n=20.\n\n## Verification\n- `tools/model_cmp.py`; geomean of per-instance ratios + bootstrap CI; cost classes separately.\n- Gate: `total_cost_usd > 0` AND non-empty `patch.diff`, dropped symmetrically.\n- `CENSORED` must stay 0 per arm.\n- Report ops (pre/post/build) alongside tokens — ops predicted tokens within 3pt in all three cells\n  above, so it is the cheap early read on whether the cell is working.\n- Verify on emitted artifacts, never config — ten silent plumbing failures this session, the worst a\n  flag checked in one dispatcher but not the one every cell uses.\n\n## Files\n- `/tmp/mcptest/s1worker.sh` (new) — the S1 flag set, Opus, cap 120.\n- `tools/run_3arm.sh` — no changes needed; `base_clamp_prompt` and `gated_clamp_eg_prompt` already\n  exist and are what S1 used.\n- `tools/model_cmp.py` — scorer, already reproduces every historical number.\n- **No product change.** `eg-consolidated` stays clean at HEAD `024f25a`; every payload-defect\n  hypothesis that would have motivated one is refuted.\n\n## Honest expectation\nThis is a port, not a theory: the config with the best fair measurement in the program, applied to the\nmodel that has never had it, with a mechanism (pre-edit halving) that is measured on 50 instances and\nabsent on Opus. That is the strongest remaining basis for the goal.\n\nThe risk is real though. Opus may simply not respond to a locate-first clamp the way Haiku does — its\nsessions are dominated by reproduce-and-verify work that the graph cannot touch, and Sonnet's own S1-\nfamily cell reached only −24.5% with pre-edit barely moving (−9%). So I would put this at\n**−20% to −35%**, wide, with a genuine chance it lands near −11% and settles the question negatively.\nThe leading indicator in step 3 means we find out cheaply rather than after 40 sessions.\n","tool_use_id":"toolu_015Vq2ovzR3QtiwMi72QSs4j"}]},"uuid":"952f5f99-e931-4161-8ea1-239f9d0f54b1","timestamp":"2026-08-03T18:23:29.614Z","toolUseResult":{"plan":"# Opus to −25% by porting S1 — the best fair config, never run on Opus\n\n## Context\n\nGoal: Opus ≥ −25% on the 20-instance official-300 sample. Current fair number **−11.2%**\n[−27.8, +9.6] (n=20, published prompt, both arms symmetric, cap 120).\n\nI stopped proposing new mechanisms and mined the existing cells. Two things came out of that, and\ntogether they change what to run.\n\n### 1. Every payload-defect hypothesis is dead — verified, not assumed\n\nA 5-agent forensic sweep read all 20 sessions and produced 38 candidate defects. Adversarial\nverifiers with a **winner-control** requirement refuted **27 of 28** adjudicated so far:\n\n| claimed defect | winner control that killed it |\n|---|---|\n| bodies printed with no line numbers (prompt promises them) | **20/20 instances** — 6/6 winners, incl. the biggest wins |\n| `impact` blind-chained to rank-1, no gate | off-gold-file target in **7/12 winners**; the two largest wins have it in *more* degenerate form |\n| query polluted by GitHub issue-template boilerplate | 2/6 control winners have boilerplate queries → boilerplate top hits |\n| hits printed out of score order | 5/20 payloads, incl. winners lucene (−52%) and vuejs (−24%) |\n| line-less fabricated `server.h` caller | redis-10095 (+65) **and valkey-1842 (−21%, a winner)** |\n| `eg-q.run` path exposed 5× in prompt | 20/20 |\n\nOnly survivor: the shared **GOMODCACHE leak** (caddy's *baseline* read the fix out of\n`~/go/pkg/mod/…caddy/v2@v2.11.4/replacer.go`, 27 of 66 calls) — and it explains a winner, not a loss