{"parentUuid":"5278a0b1-3bc5-4691-be2e-1701cb4eb2b7","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CdgPVrEr79wihNtcdXJu6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015FoNEp9xsfZUUXQRS2QyUS","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph/TOKEN-ACCURACY-PLAN.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":591,"cache_read_input_tokens":758844,"output_tokens":85,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":591,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":85,"cache_read_input_tokens":758844,"cache_creation_input_tokens":591,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":591},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdgPVjZmEDhDpmZKc5McT","type":"assistant","uuid":"57b9ff74-4a9b-4636-ae68-c36ceefb835b","timestamp":"2026-08-03T19:44:44.715Z","effort":"xhigh","session_id":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"modular-cooking-cupcake"}
{"parentUuid":"57b9ff74-4a9b-4636-ae68-c36ceefb835b","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"d38ae0e6-bc81-4a3c-b8e9-271f667a25d0","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015FoNEp9xsfZUUXQRS2QyUS","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# entire-graph — cutting tokens hard and raising accuracy\n2\t\n3\t**Diagnosed 2026-08-03** with the newest local build (`test/all-agent-fixes` @ `afd5a1c`, sha256\n4\t`f6a18d3ca7c2`), run hands-on against the repos where eg measurably loses: redis, fmtlib, gson,\n5\tlaravel, lombok, sharkdp, fastlane, apache, micropython.\n6\t\n7\tEverything below is measured on this machine. Where a number is weak or a claim is inference, it says so.\n8\t\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\t## 0. One-paragraph summary\n12\t\n13\teg's problem on the losing repos is **not retrieval recall — it is aim**. It finds the correct file\n14\t100% of the time and then points at the wrong region of it. Losers get **32.4%** of the gold-modified\n15\tlines; winners get **58.3%** — while losers pay **39% more payload bytes**. The agent receives the right\n16\tfile at the wrong offset, greps the file itself, and pays twice: once for the misleading payload, again\n17\tfor the exploration it triggered. At the previously fitted **≈9.6% tokens per residual shell call\n18\t(r = 0.753)**, closing the 26-point aim gap is the largest token lever available — and unlike every\n19\tmechanism this project has already refuted, it makes the payload **smaller**, not larger.\n20\t\n21\t---\n22\t\n23\t## 1. The measurement\n24\t\n25\tSame binary, same method, using the queries agents **actually issued** (from `gq_calls.log`), gold lines\n26\tresolved via `git apply` offsets rather than hunk headers (headers are invalid at the base commit —\n27\tfmtlib is off by +272 lines, and trusting them once already produced a retracted finding).\n28\t\n29\t| group | n | gold **FILE** found | mean gold **LINE** coverage | mean payload bytes |\n30\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n31\t| **losing instances** | 8 | **8 / 8 = 100%** | **32.4%** | 5,513 |\n32\t| **winning instances** (control) | 6 | **6 / 6 = 100%** | **58.3%** | 3,964 |\n33\t\n34\t```\n35\tLOSERS                                    WINNERS (control)\n36\tredis-10095   rank 1  cov   0%  9730 B    faker-2970      rank 1  cov 100%  7239 B\n37\tredis-10068   rank 1  cov   0%  2358 B    django-13821    rank 1  cov  50%  1784 B\n38\tredis-11510   rank 1  cov 100%  4631 B    immutable-2006  rank 2  cov 100%  5510 B\n39\tredis-11734   rank 1  cov 100%  9057 B    micropython-15898 rank 1 cov  0%  1985 B\n40\tfmtlib-2457   rank 2  cov   3%  1279 B    lucene-13170    rank 1  cov 100%  5006 B\n41\tfmtlib-1683   rank 5  cov   0%  2469 B    rubocop-13680   rank 2  cov   0%  2261 B\n42\tgson-2158     rank 1  cov   0%  8337 B\n43\tgson-2479     rank 1  cov  57%  6243 B\n44\t```\n45\t\n46\t**File coverage is 100% in both groups — it carries no signal at all.** This independently reproduces\n47\tthe one claim the published artifact marks *solid and causal* (gold line coverage, not file coverage,\n48\tseparates winners from losers, 25% vs 64%) and adds the byte dimension, which turns it from an accuracy\n49\tstory into a token story.\n50\t\n51\tHonest caveats: n=14 total; two winners (micropython-15898, rubocop-13680) sit at 0% coverage, so line\n52\tcoverage is not deterministic — the **group means** separate, individual instances do not always.\n53\t\n54\t---\n55\t\n56\t## 2. Root cause, from real payloads\n57\t\n58\t### 2a. The lexical entry point outranks the semantic callee\n59\t`redis-10095` — *\"LPOP with count returns null bulk reply instead of null array reply when key missing\"*\n60\t\n61\t- gold fix: **`src/t_list.c:501`**\n62\t- eg returned: `t_list.c:577-581` (`lpopCommand`), `t_list.c:1031-1095` (`blockingPopGenericCommand`)\n63\t\n64\tRight file **twice**, missed by 76 lines both times. `lpopCommand` matches \"LPOP\" *lexically*; the\n65\treply-shaping bug is in the **helper it calls**.\n66\t\n67\t**And the code excludes exactly that hop, on a justification the data refutes.**\n68\t`internal/sem/search_related.go:41`:\n69\t\n70\t> *\"Deliberately NOT included: outgoing CALLS. A callee is a plausible second site, but adding it as a\n71\t> fourth kind costs a slot in every rotation and the cases it reaches … are already reached as\n72\t> **co-members of the same unit**.\"*\n73\t\n74\tBut the co-member route is bounded by `searchRelatedUnitMemberLimit`, and the same file states that past\n75\tthat bound *\"the co-member route switches off entirely\"*. `t_list.c` (~1100 lines) and\n76\t`TypeAdapters.java` are far past it. So on large units **neither** route reaches the callee: outgoing\n77\tCALLS is excluded by design, and the fallback that was supposed to cover it is disabled by size. The\n78\texclusion is sound for small units and leaves a hole exactly where the losses are.\n79\t\n80\t### 2b. Structural prominence outranks the many small sites\n81\t`gson-2158` — *\"Primitive type adapters don't perform numeric conversion during serialization\"*\n82\t\n83\t- gold fix: **8 hunks** — `Gson.java:398,422` + `TypeAdapters.java:194,223,245,323,338,353`\n84\t- eg returned: `TypeAdapters.java:908` (`newFactory`), `Gson.java:240-339` (constructor)\n85\t\n86\tBoth gold files found; **every region wrong**. eg picked the factory and the constructor — high graph\n87\tcentrality — while the fix lives in six sibling adapter bodies. Because the payload emits ~one region\n88\tper file, a six-site in-file fix is **structurally unreachable**.\n89\t\n90\t### 2c. The losing repos spread across files 1.8× more often\n91\tAcross all 801 gold-patched instances:\n92\t\n93\t| | n | fix spans >1 source file | >1 hunk in one file | mean hunks |\n94\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n95\t| losing repos | 96 | **36.5%** | 53.1% | 3.0 |\n96\t| everything else | 705 | 20.4% | 44.7% | 3.1 |\n97\t\n98\tHunk *count* is identical; what differs is **spread**. With top-k 5 and one region per file, a\n99\tthree-file fix cannot fit.\n100\t\n101\t### 2d. The window machinery is not the problem — its centre is\n102\t`planSearchHeadWindow` (`search_enclosure.go:242`) already centres a window on `result.FocusLine`.\n103\tSo the fix is **not** \"emit a wider window\" — that is `--head-window-lines`, which was measured at\n104\t**+18.9 pt worse** when combined with top-k 20. The fix is to make **FocusLine land on the line that\n105\twill change**. Same bytes, different centre.\n106\t\n107\t---\n108\t\n109\t## 3. The causal chain, closed end to end\n110\t\n111\tPrior session-level work in this project fitted **tokens ≈ f(residual shell-exploration calls),\n112\tr = 0.753, ≈9.6% per call**, and found eg's own exploration is near-constant (~4–5 calls) while the\n113\t*baseline's* varies — i.e. eg pays when it fails to replace exploration. This diagnosis supplies the\n114\tmissing upstream link:\n115\t\n116\t> **wrong region** → agent greps the correctly-identified file itself → **+N residual shell calls** →\n117\t> **+9.6% tokens each** → and it has *already* paid 5.5 KB for the payload that misdirected it.\n118\t\n119\tThat is why the loss is bidirectional, and why this is a **token** lever rather than only an accuracy\n120\tone: the same change removes payload waste *and* the exploration it induces.\n121\t\n122\t---\n123\t\n124\t## 4. The plan\n125\t\n126\t### F1 — Re-centre the window on the query, not the symbol name  *(targets 2a, 2d)*\n127\tWhen a hit's enclosing symbol matches the query **lexically** (`symbol-name` / `path` / `exact-symbol`\n128\tsignals) but its **body** does not, follow **outgoing CALLS one hop**, score the callees' bodies against\n129\tthe query, and set `FocusLine` to the best-matching callee's region.\n130\t\n131\t- **Where:** focus/window selection around `planSearchHeadWindow` (`search_enclosure.go:242`) and the\n132\t  candidate scorers `symbolSearchScore` / `scoreSearchCandidates` (`search.go:4342`, `:2323`).\n133\t- **Scope the exception narrowly**, so `search_related.go:41`'s reasoning keeps holding where it is\n134\t  right: enable the callee hop **only** when the enclosing unit is past\n135\t  `searchRelatedUnitMemberLimit` (i.e. exactly where the co-member fallback is already off) **and** the\n136\t  body-match signal is absent. Small units keep today's behaviour untouched.\n137\t- **Token effect:** the window moves; it does not grow. Budget-neutral by construction.\n138\t\n139\t### F2 — Multi-span within one file when the query implies a repeated pattern  *(targets 2b, 2c)*\n140\tAllow N spans from a single file when several symbols in it score inside a narrow band (gson: six\n141\tadapter bodies all matching \"numeric conversion\"), and fund those spans from **ranks 4–5**, which the\n142\twinners' data shows are rarely the fix site.\n143\t\n144\t- **Where:** `planSearchEnclosures` / `allocateSearchSnippets` (`search_enclosure.go:144`, `:384`).\n145\t- **Constraint:** strictly budget-neutral. A version that grows the payload is disqualified before\n146\t  measurement — the winners already prove precision beats volume (3,964 B for 58% vs 5,513 B for 32%).\n147\t\n148\t### F3 — Emit line-level confidence  *(accuracy + honesty)*\n149\tThe payload asserts a region with no signal about whether the **offset** is trustworthy. When the\n150\tenclosing symbol is large and the query matched only its name/signature, say so — so the agent takes\n151\tone wider read instead of several greps. Converts a silent miss into a bounded read.\n152\t\n153\t### F4 — Retire file coverage as a metric  *(process)*\n154\tFile recall is 100% on winners **and** losers: it cannot discriminate, and payload screens of this class\n155\thave now cleared four changes that regressed at session level. Use **gold-line coverage** as the offline\n156\tdiagnostic; keep session tokens + graded resolves as the only gate.\n157\t\n158\t### Do NOT retry — measured negative in this project\n159\t`--head-window-lines` + top-k 20 (**+18.9 pt**) · reference blocks (**+14–19%**) · `EG_DEF_ONLY=1`\n160\t(**+30.5%**) · withholding declaration body slots (**+15.0%**) · whole-suite VERIFY suppression\n161\t(**+35.1%**) · raising graph usage (invariant at 1.07–1.83 calls/session across 8 configs) · any\n162\teg-only frugality prompt (unfair *and* costs resolves: 131 vs 150, p=0.0204).\n163\t\n164\t---\n165\t\n166\t## 5. Bugs found while diagnosing\n167\t\n168\t1. **41 of 112 eg graphs build DEGRADED** (DeepSWE v1.1 corpus; graphify 0/112), recorded in\n169\t   `deepswe/graphs/BUILD-MANIFEST.tsv`. Degraded graphs ship **silently** — this belongs in the payload\n170\t   so a wrong answer is attributable rather than mysterious.\n171\t2. **`entire graph version` reports `dev`** for real local builds, so benchmark cells cannot pin the\n172\t   binary by version (only sha256), and the SWE-bench ladder outright refuses `dev`.\n173\t3. **Unmerged search fixes on `codex/eg-graphify-advertised-bench`** (+14 commits, 47 files, +7052 lines):\n174\t   *\"preserve hard file limits and coverage scores\"*, *\"keep legacy eligibility before widening\"*.\n175\t   Both are directly on-topic for §2 and are **not** in the newest dev branch. **Evaluate this branch\n176\t   before writing any F1/F2 code** — it may already move line coverage.\n177\t4. **Cross-platform packaging is unguarded.** A macOS arm64 binary shipped into linux/amd64 benchmark\n178\t   containers failed `rc=126`, returned an **empty payload**, and scored as though the tool were\n179\t   worthless — a silently wrong result that looked like a real measurement. Guarded in the DeepSWE\n180\t   harness; entire-graph should publish and verify a linux/amd64 artifact.\n181\t\n182\t---\n183\t\n184\t## 6. Verification — offline first, cheap, in this order\n185\t\n186\t1. **Fix the baseline.** Run `scratchpad/diag_losers.py` over all 801 gold-patched instances with the\n187\t   newest binary to get suite-wide gold-line coverage. Current anchors: losers 32.4%, winners 58.3%.\n188\t2. **Test the codex branch against that baseline** before implementing anything (bug 3).\n189\t3. **F1/F2 acceptance:** gold-line coverage must rise at **equal-or-fewer payload bytes**, and coverage\n190\t   must rise **specifically on the losing repos** (redis/fmtlib/gson/laravel/lombok), which is where the\n191\t   26-point gap lives.\n192\t4. **Only then** a session gate: paired, **≥3 replicates**, tokens **and graded resolves**, McNemar\n193\t   non-inferiority, on a held-out set. No payload metric may gate a change (F4).\n194\t5. **Cross-model check** before shipping: the same config must be help-or-neutral on Haiku, Sonnet and\n195\t   Opus. Haiku is where eg demonstrably pays; Sonnet is where it currently costs.\n196\t\n197\t**Expected direction:** F1 and F2 remove misdirection rather than add content, so both payload bytes and\n198\tresidual exploration should fall on the same instances. Moving the losers from 32% to winner-level 58%\n199\tline coverage is the largest token lever this diagnosis found, and it is the only one on record that\n200\tmakes the payload smaller.\n201\t\n202\t**What would falsify this:** if suite-wide line coverage turns out not to differ between winners and\n203\tlosers at n≫14, or if F1/F2 raise coverage without reducing residual shell calls, then aim is not the\n204\tmechanism and this plan should be abandoned rather than tuned.\n205\t"}]},"uuid":"3cd5eedd-0922-4524-8e44-2cdd84cc39b8","timestamp":"2026-08-03T19:44:44.768Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph/TOKEN-ACCURACY-PLAN.md","content":"# entire-graph — cutting tokens hard and raising accuracy\n\n**Diagnosed 2026-08-03** with the newest local build (`test/all-agent-fixes` @ `afd5a1c`, sha256\n`f6a18d3ca7c2`), run hands-on against the repos where eg measurably loses: redis, fmtlib, gson,\nlaravel, lombok, sharkdp, fastlane, apache, micropython.\n\nEverything below is measured on this machine. Where a number is weak or a claim is inference, it says so.\n\n---\n\n## 0. One-paragraph summary\n\neg's problem on the losing repos is **not retrieval recall — it is aim**. It finds the correct file\n100% of the time and then points at the wrong region of it. Losers get **32.4%** of the gold-modified\nlines; winners get **58.3%** — while losers pay **39% more payload bytes**. The agent receives the right\nfile at the wrong offset, greps the file itself, and pays twice: once for the misleading payload, again\nfor the exploration it triggered. At the previously fitted **≈9.6% tokens per residual shell call\n(r = 0.753)**, closing the 26-point aim gap is the largest token lever available — and unlike every\nmechanism this project has already refuted, it makes the payload **smaller**, not larger.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The measurement\n\nSame binary, same method, using the queries agents **actually issued** (from `gq_calls.log`), gold lines\nresolved via `git apply` offsets rather than hunk headers (headers are invalid at the base commit —\nfmtlib is off by +272 lines, and trusting them once already produced a retracted finding).\n\n| group | n | gold **FILE** found | mean gold **LINE** coverage | mean payload bytes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **losing instances** | 8 | **8 / 8 = 100%** | **32.4%** | 5,513 |\n| **winning instances** (control) | 6 | **6 / 6 = 100%** | **58.3%** | 3,964 |\n\n```\nLOSERS                                    WINNERS (control)\nredis-10095   rank 1  cov   0%  9730 B    faker-2970      rank 1  cov 100%  7239 B\nredis-10068   rank 1  cov   0%  2358 B    django-13821    rank 1  cov  50%  1784 B\nredis-11510   rank 1  cov 100%  4631 B    immutable-2006  rank 2  cov 100%  5510 B\nredis-11734   rank 1  cov 100%  9057 B    micropython-15898 rank 1 cov  0%  1985 B\nfmtlib-2457   rank 2  cov   3%  1279 B    lucene-13170    rank 1  cov 100%  5006 B\nfmtlib-1683   rank 5  cov   0%  2469 B    rubocop-13680   rank 2  cov   0%  2261 B\ngson-2158     rank 1  cov   0%  8337 B\ngson-2479     rank 1  cov  57%  6243 B\n```\n\n**File coverage is 100% in both groups — it carries no signal at all.** This independently reproduces\nthe one claim the published artifact marks *solid and causal* (gold line coverage, not file coverage,\nseparates winners from losers, 25% vs 64%) and adds the byte dimension, which turns it from an accuracy\nstory into a token story.\n\nHonest caveats: n=14 total; two winners (micropython-15898, rubocop-13680) sit at 0% coverage, so line\ncoverage is not deterministic — the **group means** separate, individual instances do not always.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Root cause, from real payloads\n\n### 2a. The lexical entry point outranks the semantic callee\n`redis-10095` — *\"LPOP with count returns null bulk reply instead of null array reply when key missing\"*\n\n- gold fix: **`src/t_list.c:501`**\n- eg returned: `t_list.c:577-581` (`lpopCommand`), `t_list.c:1031-1095` (`blockingPopGenericCommand`)\n\nRight file **twice**, missed by 76 lines both times. `lpopCommand` matches \"LPOP\" *lexically*; the\nreply-shaping bug is in the **helper it calls**.\n\n**And the code excludes exactly that hop, on a justification the data refutes.**\n`internal/sem/search_related.go:41`:\n\n> *\"Deliberately NOT included: outgoing CALLS. A callee is a plausible second site, but adding it as a\n> fourth kind costs a slot in every rotation and the cases it reaches … are already reached as\n> **co-members of the same unit**.\"*\n\nBut the co-member route is bounded by `searchRelatedUnitMemberLimit`, and the same file states that past\nthat bound *\"the co-member route switches off entirely\"*. `t_list.c` (~1100 lines) and\n`TypeAdapters.java` are far past it. So on large units **neither** route reaches the callee: outgoing\nCALLS is excluded by design, and the fallback that was supposed to cover it is disabled by size. The\nexclusion is sound for small units and leaves a hole exactly where the losses are.\n\n### 2b. Structural prominence outranks the many small sites\n`gson-2158` — *\"Primitive type adapters don't perform numeric conversion during serialization\"*\n\n- gold fix: **8 hunks** — `Gson.java:398,422` + `TypeAdapters.java:194,223,245,323,338,353`\n- eg returned: `TypeAdapters.java:908` (`newFactory`), `Gson.java:240-339` (constructor)\n\nBoth gold files found; **every region wrong**. eg picked the factory and the constructor — high graph\ncentrality — while the fix lives in six sibling adapter bodies. Because the payload emits ~one region\nper file, a six-site in-file fix is **structurally unreachable**.\n\n### 2c. The losing repos spread across files 1.8× more often\nAcross all 801 gold-patched instances:\n\n| | n | fix spans >1 source file | >1 hunk in one file | mean hunks |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| losing repos | 96 | **36.5%** | 53.1% | 3.0 |\n| everything else | 705 | 20.4% | 44.7% | 3.1 |\n\nHunk *count* is identical; what differs is **spread**. With top-k 5 and one region per file, a\nthree-file fix cannot fit.\n\n### 2d. The window machinery is not the problem — its centre is\n`planSearchHeadWindow` (`search_enclosure.go:242`) already centres a window on `result.FocusLine`.\nSo the fix is **not** \"emit a wider window\" — that is `--head-window-lines`, which was measured at\n**+18.9 pt worse** when combined with top-k 20. The fix is to make **FocusLine land on the line that\nwill change**. Same bytes, different centre.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The causal chain, closed end to end\n\nPrior session-level work in this project fitted **tokens ≈ f(residual shell-exploration calls),\nr = 0.753, ≈9.6% per call**, and found eg's own exploration is near-constant (~4–5 calls) while the\n*baseline's* varies — i.e. eg pays when it fails to replace exploration. This diagnosis supplies the\nmissing upstream link:\n\n> **wrong region** → agent greps the correctly-identified file itself → **+N residual shell calls** →\n> **+9.6% tokens each** → and it has *already* paid 5.5 KB for the payload that misdirected it.\n\nThat is why the loss is bidirectional, and why this is a **token** lever rather than only an accuracy\none: the same change removes payload waste *and* the exploration it induces.\n\n---\n\n## 4. The plan\n\n### F1 — Re-centre the window on the query, not the symbol name  *(targets 2a, 2d)*\nWhen a hit's enclosing symbol matches the query **lexically** (`symbol-name` / `path` / `exact-symbol`\nsignals) but its **body** does not, follow **outgoing CALLS one hop**, score the callees' bodies against\nthe query, and set `FocusLine` to the best-matching callee's region.\n\n- **Where:** focus/window selection around `planSearchHeadWindow` (`search_enclosure.go:242`) and the\n  candidate scorers `symbolSearchScore` / `scoreSearchCandidates` (`search.go:4342`, `:2323`).\n- **Scope the exception narrowly**, so `search_related.go:41`'s reasoning keeps holding where it is\n  right: enable the callee hop **only** when the enclosing unit is past\n  `searchRelatedUnitMemberLimit` (i.e. exactly where the co-member fallback is already off) **and** the\n  body-match signal is absent. Small units keep today's behaviour untouched.\n- **Token effect:** the window moves; it does not grow. Budget-neutral by construction.\n\n### F2 — Multi-span within one file when the query implies a repeated pattern  *(targets 2b, 2c)*\nAllow N spans from a single file when several symbols in it score inside a narrow band (gson: six\nadapter bodies all matching \"numeric conversion\"), and fund those spans from **ranks 4–5**, which the\nwinners' data shows are rarely the fix site.\n\n- **Where:** `planSearchEnclosures` / `allocateSearchSnippets` (`search_enclosure.go:144`, `:384`).\n- **Constraint:** strictly budget-neutral. A version that grows the payload is disqualified before\n  measurement — the winners already prove precision beats volume (3,964 B for 58% vs 5,513 B for 32%).\n\n### F3 — Emit line-level confidence  *(accuracy + honesty)*\nThe payload asserts a region with no signal about whether the **offset** is trustworthy. When the\nenclosing symbol is large and the query matched only its name/signature, say so — so the agent takes\none wider read instead of several greps. Converts a silent miss into a bounded read.\n\n### F4 — Retire file coverage as a metric  *(process)*\nFile recall is 100% on winners **and** losers: it cannot discriminate, and payload screens of this class\nhave now cleared four changes that regressed at session level. Use **gold-line coverage** as the offline\ndiagnostic; keep session tokens + graded resolves as the only gate.\n\n### Do NOT retry — measured negative in this project\n`--head-window-lines` + top-k 20 (**+18.9 pt**) · reference blocks (**+14–19%**) · `EG_DEF_ONLY=1`\n(**+30.5%**) · withholding declaration body slots (**+15.0%**) · whole-suite VERIFY suppression\n(**+35.1%**) · raising graph usage (invariant at 1.07–1.83 calls/session across 8 configs) · any\neg-only frugality prompt (unfair *and* costs resolves: 131 vs 150, p=0.0204).\n\n---\n\n## 5. Bugs found while diagnosing\n\n1. **41 of 112 eg graphs build DEGRADED** (DeepSWE v1.1 corpus; graphify 0/112), recorded in\n   `deepswe/graphs/BUILD-MANIFEST.tsv`. Degraded graphs ship **silently** — this belongs in the payload\n   so a wrong answer is attributable rather than mysterious.\n2. **`entire graph version` reports `dev`** for real local builds, so benchmark cells cannot pin the\n   binary by version (only sha256), and the SWE-bench ladder outright refuses `dev`.\n3. **Unmerged search fixes on `codex/eg-graphify-advertised-bench`** (+14 commits, 47 files, +7052 lines):\n   *\"preserve hard file limits and coverage scores\"*, *\"keep legacy eligibility before widening\"*.\n   Both are directly on-topic for §2 and are **not** in the newest dev branch. **Evaluate this branch\n   before writing any F1/F2 code** — it may already move line coverage.\n4. **Cross-platform packaging is unguarded.** A macOS arm64 binary shipped into linux/amd64 benchmark\n   containers failed `rc=126`, returned an **empty payload**, and scored as though the tool were\n   worthless — a silently wrong result that looked like a real measurement. Guarded in the DeepSWE\n   harness; entire-graph should publish and verify a linux/amd64 artifact.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Verification — offline first, cheap, in this order\n\n1. **Fix the baseline.** Run `scratchpad/diag_losers.py` over all 801 gold-patched instances with the\n   newest binary to get suite-wide gold-line coverage. Current anchors: losers 32.4%, winners 58.3%.\n2. **Test the codex branch against that baseline** before implementing anything (bug 3).\n3. **F1/F2 acceptance:** gold-line coverage must rise at **equal-or-fewer payload bytes**, and coverage\n   must rise **specifically on the losing repos** (redis/fmtlib/gson/laravel/lombok), which is where the\n   26-point gap lives.\n4. **Only then** a session gate: paired, **≥3 replicates**, tokens **and graded resolves**, McNemar\n   non-inferiority, on a held-out set. No payload metric may gate a change (F4).\n5. **Cross-model check** before shipping: the same config must be help-or-neutral on Haiku, Sonnet and\n   Opus. Haiku is where eg demonstrably pays; Sonnet is where it currently costs.\n\n**Expected direction:** F1 and F2 remove misdirection rather than add content, so both payload bytes and\nresidual exploration should fall on the same instances. Moving the losers from 32% to winner-level 58%\nline coverage is the largest token lever this diagnosis found, and it is the only one on record that\nmakes the payload smaller.\n\n**What would falsify this:** if suite-wide line coverage turns out not to differ between winners and\nlosers at n≫14, or if F1/F2 raise coverage without reducing residual shell calls, then aim is not the\nmechanism and this plan should be abandoned rather than tuned.\n","numLines":205,"startLine":1,"totalLines":205}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"57b9ff74-4a9b-4636-ae68-c36ceefb835b","session_id":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"modular-cooking-cupcake"}
{"parentUuid":"3cd5eedd-0922-4524-8e44-2cdd84cc39b8","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"nested_memory","path":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph/CLAUDE.md","content":{"path":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph/CLAUDE.md","type":"Project","content":"@AGENTS.md\n","contentDiffersFromDisk":false},"displayPath":"entire-graph/CLAUDE.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"68686aac-6b1d-4dcc-b71a-5e268216074d","timestamp":"2026-08-03T19:44:44.819Z","session_id":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"modular-cooking-cupcake"}
{"parentUuid":"68686aac-6b1d-4dcc-b71a-5e268216074d","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"nested_memory","path":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph/AGENTS.md","content":{"path":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph/AGENTS.md","type":"Project","content":"# AGENTS.md — operating guide for coding agents\n\nHand this to any coding agent working in a repo where the `entire graph` plugin\nis installed. It moves initial code-location work from broad grep/read\nexploration to targeted graph queries; token impact depends on the task and\nmodel, and no end-to-end savings claim is current.\n\n## What this gives you\n\nA precomputed, **deterministic** code graph is available through the `entire graph` command — functions, classes, methods, types, routes, and the calls/inheritance/field/service relations between them, parsed with tree-sitter, 100% locally (no network, no model, no keys). Use it to **LOCATE** and **UNDERSTAND** code *before* broad grep / find / cat / whole-file exploration. Every command is no-egress and safe to run inside a sandboxed session. The same commit yields the same graph, but static relations can be heuristic or incomplete; inspect focused source and verify the resulting change.\n\nDefault flags to remember: pass `--repo .` when you're not inside an Entire session; the graph reads your **working tree by default** (your uncommitted edits are visible), and `--head` switches to committed-tree semantics with a cached, reusable index.\n\n---\n\n## The parts of the graph\n\nReach for the smallest tool that answers your question.\n\n### 🔍 search — *find the code for a task* (your first move)\nRanked source regions for a plain-language description, with the source and `file:line` inline. Hybrid ranking over bodies, identifiers (camelCase/snake_case aware), signatures, paths, and graph neighbors. Output is budgeted (16 KiB by default) to drop straight into context.\n\n```sh\nentire graph search --repo . --query \"<the task or bug in one plain sentence>\" --format text --top-k 8\n```\n\n- `--format agent` for compact ranked output with latency telemetry; `json`/`ndjson` for the full schema (completeness, partial failures, diagnostics).\n- `--top-k N` result count; `--max-context-bytes N` byte budget (`0` = unbounded).\n- Working tree by default; add `--head` for committed-tree + cache reuse.\n- `--profile syntax-only|fast|full` (default `fast`); `--index-all-files` or `--max-indexed-files N` to widen/bound cold-search parsing.\n\n**When:** the start of essentially every task. One good query lands you on the fix area.\n\n### 🕸️ neighbors — *who calls this / what does it call* (targeted relations)\nDirect incoming/outgoing relations for **one** symbol, with definition locations, plus bounded two-hop paths at `--depth 2`. For the full blast radius of a change, prefer `impact`; use `neighbors` when you want one specific relation/direction. Never `edges` for this (full stream).\n\n```sh\nentire graph neighbors --repo . --symbol NAME --relation CALLS --direction in   # who calls NAME\nentire graph neighbors --repo . --symbol NAME --relation CALLS --direction out  # what NAME calls\n```\n\n- `--file path` — **required** when the symbol name is ambiguous (multiple defs); the graph returns definitions only until you disambiguate.\n- `--relation CALLS` (default is the call family) — pick another relation to follow it instead.\n- `--direction both|in|out`, `--depth 1|2`, `--limit N`.\n- `--internal-only` drops unresolved external endpoints; `--exclude-tests` drops test-only neighbors.\n- `--format agent|text|json`; `--head` for cached committed-tree; `--profile fast` for shallow call resolution (default `full` favors correctness).\n\n**When:** \"what breaks if I change X\", \"who uses this\", tracing a call chain — after search has given you a concrete symbol name.\n\n### 💥 impact — *one-shot blast radius for a change*\nEverything the graph knows about changing **one** symbol, in a single bounded explanation: direct + transitive callers (depth ≤ 2), callees, type consumers (`USES_TYPE`/`PARAM_TYPE`/`RETURNS_TYPE`), data flows, files that historically change together with the symbol's file, and same-container siblings. Text output is sectioned, `file:line` per entry, capped per section and ~4 KB total.\n\n```sh\nentire graph impact --repo . --symbol NAME [--file path] [--depth 1|2] [--format text|json]\n```\n\n- Ambiguous names return the definition list — rerun with `--file` to pick one.\n- `--limit N` per-section entry cap; `--max-context-bytes N` total text budget; `--exclude-tests`; `--head` / `--profile` as in `neighbors`.\n\n**When:** before changing behavior of a specific function/type — \"you're changing ordering: here is every place results are ordered, limited, or consumed downstream\" — one command instead of chaining neighbors + edges + git log.\n\n### 📇 symbols — *definitions*\nFull stream of symbol records (stable `compound-v1` ID, kind, qualified name, source range, signature, language, container). This is a **bulk NDJSON stream of the whole repo**, filtered to the symbol record type — there is **no positional name argument** and no server-side name filter; grep the stream client-side, or prefer `search`/`neighbors` for a targeted single-symbol lookup.\n\n```sh\nentire graph symbols --repo . --format ndjson [--worktree]\n```\n\n**When:** you need the complete definition inventory (e.g. ingesting into a store), not a single lookup.\n\n### 🔗 edges — *relations*\nFull stream of relation records across all 30 types (`CALLS`, `IMPORTS`, `EXTENDS`, `HANDLES_ROUTE`, …), each tagged with resolution and confidence. Like `symbols`, this is the **whole-repo stream** — there is **no `--to`/`--from`/`--relation` filter**; for one symbol's callers/callees use `neighbors`, not `edges`.\n\n```sh\nentire graph edges --repo . --format ndjson [--worktree]\n```\n\n**When:** you want every relation (bulk export / ingestion). For a targeted question, use `neighbors`.\n\n### 🗺️ snapshot — *the whole graph*\nOne header record, then file, external-endpoint, symbol, and relation records, streamed so memory stays bounded. Superset of `symbols` + `edges` + files.\n\n```sh\nentire graph snapshot --repo . --format ndjson [--worktree]\n```\n\n**When:** ingesting the full graph into agent memory or a store such as Entire Brain.\n\n### 🧬 diff / analyze / commit / checkpoint — *what changed + risk*\nEntity-level change list (added / removed / renamed / signature-changed / body-changed) with a heuristic **dependent count**, so a signature change with many dependents stands out.\n\n```sh\nentire graph commit HEAD --json                     # a commit vs its first parent\nentire graph diff --base main --head HEAD --json    # between two refs (analyze is an alias)\nentire graph checkpoint <id> --json                 # the commit behind an Entire-Checkpoint trailer\n```\n\n**When:** judging whether a change is safe to keep / revert / continue, or reviewing a branch/PR. High dependent counts on a signature change = run tests first.\n\n### 🏗️ index — *build / warm the cache*\nPrebuilds the durable, query-independent committed-tree index and verifies it was written, before latency-sensitive work.\n\n```sh\nentire graph index --repo . --head --profile full --cache-dir /path/to/cache --format json\n```\n\n**When:** once, up front, on a large repo before a batch of `--head` searches/neighbors queries. Re-running it is also how you \"refresh\" a committed-tree cache — same tree hits, changed tree rebuilds.\n\n### 🧭 capabilities / doctor / version — *feature-detect*\n```sh\nentire graph capabilities --json    # semantic vs inventory-only languages, relation types, features\nentire graph doctor --json          # environment, repo resolution, no_egress=true\nentire graph version [--json]       # provider name + plugin version\n```\n\n**When:** before assuming a language is semantically parsed, or to confirm the no-egress environment.\n\n### 📊 stats — *did the graph actually save anything?* (for humans, not for you)\n```sh\nentire graph stats --repo . [--since 30d|7d|all] [--format text|json] [--sessions-dir path|--transcript path]\n```\n\nLocal, read-only report over the coding-agent session transcripts already on disk\n(`~/.claude/projects/<path-slug>/*.jsonl`; `--sessions-dir` overrides the lookup). Reports graph\ncalls per verb vs. exploration calls (`Read` whole-file / `Read` line-range / `Grep` / `Glob` /\nshell `grep|find|cat|head|tail|sed|awk`), the bytes each path pulled into context, billed session\ntokens read from transcript `usage`, a graph-first rate (share of sessions whose first locate-ish\ntool call was a graph call), and an **estimated** token saving. The savings model is an explicit\nassumption printed next to the number: each `search`/`neighbors`/`impact` call is credited with the\none whole-file read it replaced — on-disk size of the top-hit file it pointed at (repo median\ntracked-file size when unresolvable) minus the bytes that call returned, floored at 0, at 4 bytes =\n1 token. It is not a measured counterfactual. No network, no writes. `--transcript <path>` narrows\nthe whole report to one session (that transcript plus its `<session>/subagents/*.jsonl`), which is\nwhat `scripts/entire-graph-statusline.sh` renders as a live Claude Code status line badge.\n\n**When:** a human asks what the graph is buying them. Agents should not run it as part of a coding task.\n\n---\n\n## Resolution-first agent prompt (copy-paste this)\n\nThe former “measured-best” early-stop prompt and its 54.9%/57.7% token claims\nare withdrawn. It optimized token use without preserving resolution parity and\ncould reward a cheap wrong patch. Use this correctness-first guidance instead;\nsubstitute your search invocation for `<search-cmd>`:\n\n```text\nA precomputed code-search tool is available: <search-cmd> . Use it to LOCATE the fix BEFORE any\ngrep/find. Your FIRST action must be ONE search:\n  <search-cmd> \"<the bug in one sentence>\"   <-- ranked relevant code (file:line + source)\nThen open the top hit's file with your native Read tool (pass a line range around the reported\nline), inspect enough surrounding behavior to justify the change, and make the smallest complete\nedit. Treat graph output as evidence, not an oracle. Check callers/impact when the change can affect\nother sites. If the result prints a `VERIFY:` line, run that exact command after editing before\nclaiming the task is done; it may be a per-file command or a whole-suite fallback when no narrow\ncommand can be derived. Never report that tests pass without executing the command. If verification\ncannot be run, state why and perform a bounded source-level check. Optimize turns only after\ncorrectness.\n```\n\nFor bug-fix/locate tasks, run search at `--profile full` (call-graph expansion active) with default\ntext output (tiered: full snippet for the top hits, terse locators after). Prefer\ntargeted follow-up queries over whole-graph dumps, but use the graph and source\nchecks needed to make and verify a complete fix.\n\n## Operating doctrine\n\n1. **Search first for location tasks.** Start with one `entire graph search --query \"<task>\"` before broad grep/find exploration.\n2. **Treat results as evidence, not truth.** Read focused source around the result and widen the check when behavior, aliases, generated code, or dynamic dispatch could matter.\n3. **Use graph follow-ups when they answer a real question.** `impact`, `callers`, and `neighbors` are appropriate for blast radius and related-site checks; avoid exploratory whole-graph dumps.\n4. **Make the smallest complete change.** Check sibling sites and contracts when the task implies them.\n5. **Verify before stopping.** Run the `VERIFY:` command when search prints one; it may be a whole-suite fallback when no narrow command exists. Never report that tests pass without having run them. If execution is unavailable, perform a bounded source-level verification and disclose the limitation.\n6. **Optimize context after correctness.** Prefer precise queries and line ranges, but never trade resolution for fewer turns.\n7. **Feature-detect before you trust.** If a language might be inventory-only, check `capabilities --json` first — inventory-only files have file records but no semantic relations.\n\nQuick mental model:\n\n```text\nlocate  →  entire graph search --query \"...\"          (ranked code + file:line)\nimpact  →  entire graph impact --symbol X              (one-shot blast radius: callers, types, data flow, co-change)\ncallers →  entire graph neighbors --symbol X ...       (targeted callers/callees of X)\nchange  →  entire graph diff --base A --head B          (entity-level, with dependents)\ningest  →  entire graph snapshot --format ndjson        (whole graph)\nreport  →  entire graph stats --repo .                  (human-facing: graph vs grep/read usage + estimated token savings)\n```\n\n---\n\n## Working on entire-graph itself\n\nIf your task is modifying this repository (not just using it), the build/test surface is in `mise.toml`:\n\n```sh\nmise run build   # go build -o entire-graph ./cmd/entire-graph  (needs CGO for tree-sitter)\nmise run test    # go test ./...\nmise run check   # fmt + vet + race tests + build\n```\n\nContract rules that must not break: schema `1.x` is frozen and additive-only (`docs/adr/0001-ga-schema-contract.md`); the provider is **no-egress** (never add remote fetches, hosted API calls, telemetry, or runtime grammar downloads); `compound-v1` symbol IDs must stay stable across ordinary edits; unsupported/unparseable files must surface as machine-readable partial failures, never silent drops. All logic lives under `internal/` (`sem` = parsing/graph/search, `cli` = hand-rolled dispatch, `gitutil` = git subprocess); `cmd/entire-graph/main.go` is a thin entry point. The plugin manifest (`entire-plugin.yml`) registers the subcommand `graph`, so users type `entire graph ...`. This project was **previously named `entire-sem`** — do not reintroduce the old name. **Entire Brain** (`entire-brain`) is the separate downstream consumer of this provider's NDJSON — not an old name for this project.\n","contentDiffersFromDisk":false,"parent":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph/CLAUDE.md"},"displayPath":"entire-graph/AGENTS.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"395fe6b1-c3c4-4d8b-a167-338ff25f6457","timestamp":"2026-08-03T19:44:44.820Z","session_id":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"modular-cooking-cupcake"}
