{"parentUuid":"b6f72043-b6c2-4eb2-958d-f01729cc4ac4","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CdgQqo4mjtyjqWQJ5B5G2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Running in the background — 10 rep30 instances where I already know the token outcome, split 6 winners / 4 losers.\n\n**What this tests.** The plan's central claim is that gold-**line** coverage separates winners from losers (32.4% vs 58.3%) while file coverage doesn't. If that split reproduces on my corpus — where I have measured token ratios for the same instances — then aim is the mechanism and F1 is worth implementing. If coverage comes out flat across my winners and losers, the plan's premise doesn't transfer, and per its own falsification clause it *\"should be abandoned rather than tuned.\"*\n\nThree things now settled, worth separating from what isn't:\n\n**Settled.** The codex branch does not move line coverage — clean A/B on the same codebase, base binary sha `f6a18d3ca7c2` matching the plan's stated binary exactly. So it doesn't subsume F1, and the plan's step-2 gate is cleared.\n\n**Settled.** My gold-line resolver is correct. Verified against patch shapes: lucene 1 hunk / 1 added / 1 removed = 2 lines, django 4 + 2 = 6. Lucene's gold sits at line 148 while the payload's top span is 74–94, so 0% is the right answer for that query on that commit.\n\n**Not settled.** Whether the coverage split is real on my corpus. The plan and I agree on every loser we share and disagree on the winner, which is exactly the pattern that would make the \"26-point gap\" smaller than advertised. Its corpus is DeepSWE v1.1 with different instances and different recorded queries, so this is a genuine transfer question, not a bookkeeping one.\n\nMeanwhile rep30 sits at **−13.9% [−35.3, +13.2]**, n=10 of 30, 21 of 60 jobs done — and I'll report that cell only when it completes, given how badly its interims have misled."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"s