{"parentUuid":"e5fb70fa-7337-4cc5-9dff-be1545b70758","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CdgR2UJUK9gCV6ECAQyiZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Update — 24 of 60 done, n=11 gated: −10.5% [−31.8, +15.7], winners 6/11.**\n\nDrifting down as losers land: −40.9% (n=4) → −24.6% (n=8) → −13.9% (n=10) → **−10.5% (n=11)**. hugo-12562 came in at +32%. The cell is converging on roughly the same place as the official 20 (−11.2%), which undercuts the instance-set hypothesis — rep30's baselines aren't systematically wastier after all, at least not under the published prompt.\n\nThe six winners are unchanged and clean-looking (lucene −58%, caddy −52%, carbon-3098 −39%, django −35%, docusaurus-9183 −19%, ruff −7%), and the five losses are all instances the official 20 doesn't contain.\n\nWorth connecting to what I just measured: **every one of those six winners has ~0% gold-line coverage.** lucene wins by 58% while the payload prints four spans from the correct file (34-36, 27, 181-188, 118-124) and the gold line at 148 falls between them. So the wins are not coming from aim — they're coming from the baseline being wasteful on those instances, exactly as the break-even law says (`eg_ops = 14.16 + 0.588·base_ops`, break-even 34.4).\n\nThat's the cleanest statement of where the program stands: the tool currently wins by *replacing a wasteful baseline*, not by aiming well, and it aims badly on 100% of instances measured. Those are independent facts, and the second one is a real untried lever — which is why the aim defect matters even though the plan's sizing argument for it doesn't hold on my corpus.\n\n36 jobs left. I'll report the completed cell rather than more interims."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":993,"cache_read_input_tokens":806982,"output_tokens":582,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_request